{"name":"Wool Tweed","hex":"#917747","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hair of the sheep, llama and some other ruminants.","A cloth or yarn made from such hair."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wool"},{"term":"tweed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coarse woolen fabric used for clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tweed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wool","blocks":[{"text":"Wool is the textile fiber obtained from sheep and other mammals, especially goats, rabbits, and camelids. The term may also refer to inorganic materials, such as mineral wool and glass wool, that have some properties similar to animal wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/330px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wool"},{"title":"Tweed","blocks":[{"text":"Tweed is a rough, woollen fabric, of a soft, open, flexible texture, resembling cheviot or homespun, but more closely woven. It is usually woven with a plain weave, twill or herringbone structure. Colour effects in the yarn may be obtained by mixing dyed wool before it is spun."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Harris_tweed.jpg/330px-Harris_tweed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweed"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg/500px-Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg/1280px-Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo